WEBVTT

1
00:01:23,042 --> 00:01:28,005
THE NOT TOO DISTANT FUTURE...

2
00:02:34,488 --> 00:02:35,906
LEMON ART SUPPLIES

3
00:03:01,974 --> 00:03:03,225
How's it going?

4
00:03:03,976 --> 00:03:05,603
Did they decide on their land?

5
00:03:06,061 --> 00:03:09,398
Scouting more today. Near Ofuna.

6
00:03:10,107 --> 00:03:11,609
Takano...

7
00:03:12,776 --> 00:03:15,279
I built one there. Single family home.

8
00:03:15,863 --> 00:03:20,242
Beside Tamonin Temple.
A proper two story.

9
00:03:20,242 --> 00:03:21,702
Might be nearby.

10
00:03:22,328 --> 00:03:24,622
I wish they'd make up their minds.

11
00:03:27,291 --> 00:03:28,292
Othello?

12
00:03:30,461 --> 00:03:31,837
Othello?

13
00:03:37,593 --> 00:03:39,261
Missing?

14
00:03:41,180 --> 00:03:43,015
It's been a week.

15
00:03:44,058 --> 00:03:45,851
Maybe he found a girlfriend.

16
00:03:46,602 --> 00:03:47,937
I wish.

17
00:03:48,646 --> 00:03:50,898
He'll get dumped and come home.

18
00:04:36,110 --> 00:04:38,362
You want more time together.

19
00:04:38,362 --> 00:04:39,613
You're not ready to forget.

20
00:04:39,613 --> 00:04:40,572
DON'T FORGET ME

21
00:04:40,572 --> 00:04:42,199
At REbirth,

22
00:04:42,199 --> 00:04:45,452
we sympathize with
and support these feelings.

23
00:05:35,169 --> 00:05:36,754
Good morning.

24
00:05:40,632 --> 00:05:41,842
Go ahead.

25
00:05:41,842 --> 00:05:43,260
Departing.

26
00:05:45,137 --> 00:05:47,556
Thank you!

27
00:06:03,072 --> 00:06:04,114
LAND FOR SALE

28
00:06:04,114 --> 00:06:05,866
What was here before?

29
00:06:05,866 --> 00:06:07,993
It was always woodland.

30
00:06:07,993 --> 00:06:09,078
I see.

31
00:06:09,078 --> 00:06:13,457
Is it a little too steep
to build a house?

32
00:06:14,291 --> 00:06:19,421
But the light is fantastic.
And it's so quiet.

33
00:06:19,421 --> 00:06:21,632
Is the sea this way?

34
00:06:22,049 --> 00:06:26,136
Yes, it's over
the Rokkoku Kenzan Mountain.

35
00:06:26,136 --> 00:06:27,429
Oh, this way.

36
00:06:27,429 --> 00:06:30,933
It's scary by the sea, tsunamis...

37
00:06:30,933 --> 00:06:34,019
I like this better than Fujisawa.

38
00:06:34,019 --> 00:06:37,231
-Twelve minutes to the station...
-Look, a cat!

39
00:06:37,231 --> 00:06:39,274
Too far to hear the waves...

40
00:06:39,274 --> 00:06:42,986
The living room should be
12 tatami mat size.

41
00:06:42,986 --> 00:06:44,822
Can you build this far?

42
00:06:44,822 --> 00:06:47,032
That big? We're a family of four.

43
00:06:47,616 --> 00:06:53,288
If the cliff regulations allow it,
a pillar here, extend the floor,

44
00:06:53,288 --> 00:06:56,208
and make a large balcony.

45
00:06:56,208 --> 00:06:59,753
Windows here,
avoiding neighbors views.

46
00:07:04,883 --> 00:07:08,345
This one here, I really like it.

47
00:07:08,345 --> 00:07:09,888
Oh, this one.

48
00:07:09,888 --> 00:07:12,474
Did I know about this?

49
00:07:12,474 --> 00:07:14,893
-We talked about it.
-No, we didn't.

50
00:07:14,893 --> 00:07:17,437
Can you build this there?

51
00:07:17,437 --> 00:07:20,649
The plot is large enough.

52
00:07:20,649 --> 00:07:24,361
Make the windows large
to let in the light.

53
00:07:24,361 --> 00:07:26,989
Some Japanese touches in the interiors.

54
00:07:26,989 --> 00:07:28,949
Is this Spain? Italy?

55
00:07:28,949 --> 00:07:32,744
His mother's paying for it,
because she'll move in.

56
00:07:34,204 --> 00:07:37,791
It's amazing, but isn't it
beyond our budget?

57
00:07:37,791 --> 00:07:44,506
A Japanese room
with 4.5 tatami mats here, with the view.

58
00:07:44,506 --> 00:07:45,340
Oh, that...

59
00:07:47,926 --> 00:07:49,469
He puts stickers everywhere.

60
00:07:49,469 --> 00:07:50,721
How old is he?

61
00:07:51,680 --> 00:07:53,223
Seven...

62
00:07:53,223 --> 00:07:55,100
Two years older than ours.

63
00:07:55,559 --> 00:07:58,896
In the children's rooms,
white walls will get dirty.

64
00:07:58,896 --> 00:08:01,940
The kids' rooms come later.

65
00:08:01,940 --> 00:08:03,692
First, exteriors.

66
00:08:05,444 --> 00:08:07,029
Orientation?

67
00:08:07,029 --> 00:08:08,071
Yeah...

68
00:08:09,740 --> 00:08:13,035
REbirth, being reborn.

69
00:08:14,995 --> 00:08:17,497
Right, from Kakeru's funeral.

70
00:08:18,498 --> 00:08:20,751
Yeah, that's right.

71
00:08:21,835 --> 00:08:24,463
This suddenly arrived this morning.

72
00:08:24,463 --> 00:08:28,008
They're hyenas. Cashing in on misfortune.

73
00:08:28,592 --> 00:08:30,427
Don't be cruel.

74
00:08:31,094 --> 00:08:32,971
Did your clients ask you?

75
00:08:32,971 --> 00:08:35,390
"You're the Komoto from that incident?"

76
00:08:35,390 --> 00:08:37,184
That's enough for today.

77
00:08:37,184 --> 00:08:38,560
Okay, got it.

78
00:08:38,560 --> 00:08:40,229
They didn't know...

79
00:08:40,646 --> 00:08:41,897
About what?

80
00:08:43,357 --> 00:08:44,775
About the accident.

81
00:08:45,734 --> 00:08:49,238
She saw the train sticker
and asked me his age.

82
00:08:50,364 --> 00:08:51,531
So, that's good.

83
00:08:53,575 --> 00:08:54,868
Not good?

84
00:08:56,411 --> 00:08:58,538
It's only been two years.

85
00:09:00,415 --> 00:09:02,542
They're not locals.

86
00:09:04,253 --> 00:09:06,964
Ofuna is the next town.

87
00:09:08,757 --> 00:09:13,345
That's how it is
for someone else's child.

88
00:09:20,310 --> 00:09:22,688
What matters is we remember.

89
00:09:23,605 --> 00:09:25,816
And now Othello's gone...

90
00:09:25,816 --> 00:09:27,985
This isn't about Othello.

91
00:09:30,862 --> 00:09:33,240
He liked Kakeru so much...

92
00:09:33,240 --> 00:09:34,908
You want more time--

93
00:09:44,626 --> 00:09:46,628
Japanese taste, huh?

94
00:09:46,628 --> 00:09:51,008
Sliding doors?
Sandy finish wallpaper?

95
00:09:51,008 --> 00:09:56,346
Custom-designed homes are scarce.
Give them special care.

96
00:09:57,347 --> 00:10:00,434
-But more important...
-That's important.

97
00:10:00,434 --> 00:10:02,894
He was in my dream this morning.

98
00:10:03,770 --> 00:10:07,899
He seemed so happy.

99
00:10:09,776 --> 00:10:12,237
I'm only agreeing to hear them out.

100
00:10:12,654 --> 00:10:14,281
Third time.

101
00:10:21,496 --> 00:10:25,959
You had a long drive. Are you tired?

102
00:10:33,675 --> 00:10:37,512
This area is known for luna moths,
our symbol.

103
00:10:37,888 --> 00:10:39,556
Luna moths.

104
00:10:39,556 --> 00:10:43,518
Legends say they carry
messages from the dead.

105
00:10:45,103 --> 00:10:47,773
We're now providing a service

106
00:10:47,773 --> 00:10:51,735
for families who lost loved ones
to accidents or crimes.

107
00:10:52,069 --> 00:10:56,573
A complimentary rental
of our latest edition humanoid.

108
00:11:01,620 --> 00:11:02,871
So, it's free?

109
00:11:05,540 --> 00:11:08,794
The humanoids we provide are a miracle,

110
00:11:08,794 --> 00:11:15,217
made possible by the latest
generative AI and robotics engineering.

111
00:11:17,177 --> 00:11:20,055
When you can't accept
their sudden passing,

112
00:11:20,055 --> 00:11:23,475
when you want to
spend more time together,

113
00:11:25,310 --> 00:11:28,688
we utilize your family's
audio and video data,

114
00:11:29,981 --> 00:11:34,027
so your kindhearted daughter,
your adorable son,

115
00:11:34,027 --> 00:11:35,695
your dependable father,

116
00:11:35,695 --> 00:11:42,202
can be reborn and
tenderly stay by your side.

117
00:11:43,662 --> 00:11:48,875
Currently, our 3,000 friends
are helping many families

118
00:11:48,875 --> 00:11:53,213
find happiness in their homes.

119
00:11:56,049 --> 00:11:57,300
A new tomorrow

120
00:11:57,300 --> 00:11:59,594
with your humanoid.

121
00:11:59,594 --> 00:12:02,055
Like a psychic's sales pitch...

122
00:12:07,644 --> 00:12:10,480
People's misfortunes can be profitable.

123
00:12:10,480 --> 00:12:11,898
Hey...

124
00:12:18,780 --> 00:12:21,575
I told you I don't like that.

125
00:12:21,575 --> 00:12:22,701
This?

126
00:12:23,618 --> 00:12:26,705
It tastes better this way. Try a bite?

127
00:12:29,082 --> 00:12:30,333
Fighting?

128
00:12:33,753 --> 00:12:35,130
We're fine.

129
00:12:35,505 --> 00:12:37,007
What is that?

130
00:12:39,050 --> 00:12:41,428
Cod roe spaghetti, I think.

131
00:12:42,554 --> 00:12:44,598
Not spicy cod roe?

132
00:12:47,392 --> 00:12:49,436
You like spicy cod roe?

133
00:12:50,437 --> 00:12:54,232
I'm a humanoid, so I can't eat.

134
00:12:59,321 --> 00:13:00,864
That's too bad.

135
00:13:01,573 --> 00:13:02,699
Itsuki.

136
00:13:04,034 --> 00:13:05,160
Excuse me.

137
00:13:06,119 --> 00:13:08,371
Don't go far.

138
00:13:08,371 --> 00:13:10,665
That's not beef...

139
00:13:10,665 --> 00:13:15,045
We came for maintenance
but he seems to like it here.

140
00:13:16,963 --> 00:13:18,465
How long ago?

141
00:13:19,090 --> 00:13:20,258
Did...

142
00:13:20,258 --> 00:13:22,093
It's been two years.

143
00:13:24,387 --> 00:13:26,640
It was a car accident...

144
00:13:26,640 --> 00:13:29,768
I just couldn't accept it.

145
00:13:31,019 --> 00:13:32,938
Isn't it difficult?

146
00:13:38,818 --> 00:13:42,447
So many things
I wanted to do for Itsuki.

147
00:13:43,073 --> 00:13:45,450
It's what I live for now.

148
00:13:49,079 --> 00:13:50,705
The fountain!

149
00:13:53,833 --> 00:13:55,168
Ouch.

150
00:13:55,669 --> 00:13:57,212
Excuse me.

151
00:13:58,046 --> 00:14:01,007
Oh, no, are you okay?

152
00:14:02,884 --> 00:14:04,553
You hit your head.

153
00:14:05,762 --> 00:14:09,474
Could you tell? That he's not human?

154
00:14:10,392 --> 00:14:11,893
Not at all.

155
00:14:14,062 --> 00:14:16,189
Brings back memories...

156
00:14:18,525 --> 00:14:20,235
Tokyo Tower, right?

157
00:14:20,986 --> 00:14:22,279
Yeah.

158
00:14:22,737 --> 00:14:24,573
Kakeru hit his head,

159
00:14:24,573 --> 00:14:28,326
dropped his soft cream
and messed up here.

160
00:14:28,326 --> 00:14:30,620
Yes, it was matcha flavor.

161
00:14:30,620 --> 00:14:33,456
We got him a weird shirt at the shop.

162
00:14:34,958 --> 00:14:37,627
I wonder where I put it.

163
00:15:04,321 --> 00:15:05,572
His third year celebration.

164
00:15:07,699 --> 00:15:08,783
Here.

165
00:15:10,577 --> 00:15:11,911
Thanks.

166
00:15:12,954 --> 00:15:14,789
Only sending smiling ones?

167
00:15:15,498 --> 00:15:18,418
You don't usually send crying videos.

168
00:15:20,128 --> 00:15:23,673
Hey, looks a little unstable to me.

169
00:15:24,299 --> 00:15:25,508
Okay.

170
00:15:29,179 --> 00:15:31,097
Helps to have a shaved head.

171
00:15:33,350 --> 00:15:35,352
He can't get into water.

172
00:15:35,352 --> 00:15:40,023
None in the sea, pool or the bath.

173
00:15:41,524 --> 00:15:44,569
No eating scenes either.

174
00:15:49,908 --> 00:15:51,326
Trains?

175
00:15:52,535 --> 00:15:53,995
Let's not.

176
00:15:55,121 --> 00:15:57,916
Get him all new clothes.

177
00:15:58,291 --> 00:16:00,835
Store his old ones away.

178
00:16:02,379 --> 00:16:06,007
Better he arrive by car
than in a box, right?

179
00:16:13,515 --> 00:16:16,518
Seven years... eight years...

180
00:16:16,518 --> 00:16:18,978
nine years old is too big.

181
00:16:25,944 --> 00:16:28,029
Say, come...

182
00:16:34,494 --> 00:16:37,038
Shall we choose yellow?

183
00:16:37,038 --> 00:16:38,123
His favorite color.

184
00:16:38,832 --> 00:16:41,960
But it's hard to match the bottoms.

185
00:16:44,671 --> 00:16:47,215
This'll look good, right?

186
00:16:47,674 --> 00:16:50,760
You're right. So, that and...

187
00:16:52,095 --> 00:16:54,013
these pants for the bottoms.

188
00:16:54,639 --> 00:16:56,808
They were expensive.

189
00:16:56,808 --> 00:17:00,228
I bought them at Isetan last month.

190
00:17:01,187 --> 00:17:03,690
He deserves Isetan clothes.

191
00:17:29,132 --> 00:17:31,259
Othello...

192
00:17:31,676 --> 00:17:33,887
You're not Othello.

193
00:17:41,394 --> 00:17:42,979
Othello...

194
00:17:43,813 --> 00:17:46,441
-Sorry.
-Why beer?

195
00:17:46,441 --> 00:17:49,068
Store's far, lukewarm... Oh, no!

196
00:17:50,153 --> 00:17:52,864
-It's a beer.
-A beer.

197
00:17:52,864 --> 00:17:55,950
-Oh, I screwed up.
-Stop playacting.

198
00:17:56,576 --> 00:17:58,036
What a waste!

199
00:17:58,536 --> 00:18:01,623
-Can you drive me home...
-Drink it at home.

200
00:18:01,623 --> 00:18:03,124
Boss...

201
00:18:05,460 --> 00:18:08,046
-Shouldn't you go home?
-Well...

202
00:18:11,090 --> 00:18:12,675
You don't want to.

203
00:18:13,176 --> 00:18:14,761
Shut up, idiot.

204
00:18:15,512 --> 00:18:16,554
I see.

205
00:18:17,263 --> 00:18:18,890
You don't want to go home.

206
00:18:20,225 --> 00:18:21,684
Boss...

207
00:18:26,481 --> 00:18:29,609
Let's go to Pachinko instead.

208
00:18:29,609 --> 00:18:30,652
I will not!

209
00:18:32,111 --> 00:18:34,155
It's not Pachinko's fault.

210
00:18:34,989 --> 00:18:36,866
Then who's fault is it?

211
00:18:40,036 --> 00:18:42,330
It's not anybody's fault.

212
00:18:44,666 --> 00:18:47,418
It's nobody's fault a child died?

213
00:18:55,510 --> 00:18:56,427
Look!

214
00:18:57,262 --> 00:18:59,055
-Is that it?
-Yeah.

215
00:19:14,821 --> 00:19:16,823
He's in the passenger seat.

216
00:19:20,994 --> 00:19:23,329
That's where he always sat.

217
00:19:23,329 --> 00:19:24,664
You're right.

218
00:19:26,249 --> 00:19:29,794
I'm glad the shirt looks good on him.

219
00:19:45,351 --> 00:19:48,396
Welcome home... Kakeru.

220
00:19:49,063 --> 00:19:51,816
I'm home... Mama.

221
00:19:52,692 --> 00:19:53,860
Papa.

222
00:19:55,445 --> 00:19:58,698
Let's go inside.

223
00:20:00,658 --> 00:20:02,493
It's been a while.

224
00:20:03,703 --> 00:20:05,538
Okay, watch your step.

225
00:20:06,164 --> 00:20:07,540
Here it is.

226
00:20:08,207 --> 00:20:09,834
You came home.

227
00:20:10,627 --> 00:20:11,961
This is yours.

228
00:20:12,879 --> 00:20:17,800
"When 30 meters away from its owner,
power will auto-off."

229
00:20:18,509 --> 00:20:24,098
"Use this setting to check GPS
by mobile phone."

230
00:20:24,933 --> 00:20:28,061
"Intelligence level can be selected."

231
00:20:28,770 --> 00:20:31,606
Shall we start at age seven?

232
00:20:32,732 --> 00:20:35,234
It has been set at age seven.

233
00:20:35,234 --> 00:20:39,948
"Talk to it a lot.
It will respond with healthy growth."

234
00:20:39,948 --> 00:20:42,867
-It's a Tamagotchi, remember?
-Ken...

235
00:20:44,786 --> 00:20:46,204
The small one.

236
00:20:47,288 --> 00:20:49,874
"Power button on back of neck."

237
00:20:52,085 --> 00:20:55,046
Here it is.
"Press and hold to turn it off."

238
00:20:56,297 --> 00:21:01,010
"When battery charge dips below 20%,
it will walk" to this...

239
00:21:01,636 --> 00:21:04,555
Kakeru, charging chair.

240
00:21:08,768 --> 00:21:10,603
Oh, this is where it appears.

241
00:21:11,062 --> 00:21:12,939
Now charging.

242
00:21:12,939 --> 00:21:15,775
-It can switch to solar.
-It's a Roomba.

243
00:21:17,902 --> 00:21:19,028
Where are you going?

244
00:21:19,529 --> 00:21:21,030
A game.

245
00:21:21,030 --> 00:21:24,200
I told you, semifinals.

246
00:21:24,909 --> 00:21:26,828
A baseball game, today?

247
00:21:27,745 --> 00:21:31,457
You had him delivered
on a game day.

248
00:21:32,834 --> 00:21:34,502
Game day was set first.

249
00:21:40,508 --> 00:21:42,802
Welcome home... Kakeru.

250
00:21:43,469 --> 00:21:46,097
I'm home... Mama.

251
00:21:50,018 --> 00:21:51,602
I'm home.

252
00:21:52,020 --> 00:21:54,647
We lost because of Gen.

253
00:22:14,834 --> 00:22:15,960
Hey.

254
00:22:18,296 --> 00:22:20,673
Oh, what is it?

255
00:22:20,673 --> 00:22:23,259
No, I'm just home.

256
00:22:24,218 --> 00:22:25,261
What?

257
00:22:26,721 --> 00:22:28,514
What time is it?

258
00:22:28,514 --> 00:22:31,100
Seven o'clock at night.

259
00:22:31,100 --> 00:22:33,519
What? Already?

260
00:22:34,187 --> 00:22:37,774
Oto... All day, since morning?

261
00:22:38,316 --> 00:22:39,400
Yeah.

262
00:22:40,234 --> 00:22:41,694
The two of us.

263
00:22:43,112 --> 00:22:44,197
Right?

264
00:22:50,828 --> 00:22:53,790
-What about dinner?
-Don't say dinner now!

265
00:22:53,790 --> 00:22:56,000
Kakeru can't eat.

266
00:22:56,584 --> 00:22:57,752
Right.

267
00:22:59,420 --> 00:23:01,255
Go out to eat.

268
00:23:02,673 --> 00:23:03,966
And you?

269
00:23:04,592 --> 00:23:06,469
I won't eat either.

270
00:23:08,888 --> 00:23:10,389
I'm eating...

271
00:23:12,517 --> 00:23:15,645
Because I'm a human being.
If I don't eat, I'll die.

272
00:23:16,479 --> 00:23:18,481
-But first, a bath...
-Shh!

273
00:23:39,210 --> 00:23:40,670
Welcome home.

274
00:23:42,505 --> 00:23:44,257
I'm home.

275
00:23:51,722 --> 00:23:53,224
Welcome home.

276
00:23:53,975 --> 00:23:55,643
I'm home.

277
00:24:34,599 --> 00:24:36,017
I'm home.

278
00:24:40,396 --> 00:24:42,023
Welcome.

279
00:25:01,792 --> 00:25:03,753
You're Papa, right?

280
00:25:06,923 --> 00:25:09,759
I'm not your papa.

281
00:25:10,509 --> 00:25:14,096
So, don't call me that.

282
00:25:22,271 --> 00:25:24,148
Call me mister.

283
00:25:25,983 --> 00:25:27,443
Mister?

284
00:25:28,319 --> 00:25:29,403
Yeah.

285
00:25:32,990 --> 00:25:35,326
Why aren't you my papa?

286
00:25:37,036 --> 00:25:41,123
Because I'm not your papa.

287
00:25:48,839 --> 00:25:50,925
Are you the stubborn type?

288
00:25:54,595 --> 00:25:59,684
I get it, amazing.

289
00:25:59,684 --> 00:26:03,688
Here's the Japanese room
off the entryway.

290
00:26:04,188 --> 00:26:07,108
It looks like this.

291
00:26:07,733 --> 00:26:10,069
Connected to a terrace.

292
00:26:10,569 --> 00:26:12,321
You can see this, too.

293
00:26:12,321 --> 00:26:15,366
They found cancer in the pancreas.

294
00:26:15,366 --> 00:26:16,158
What?

295
00:26:17,201 --> 00:26:19,578
Not me, my mother.

296
00:26:20,538 --> 00:26:24,375
So I quickly decided
she should move in with us.

297
00:26:24,875 --> 00:26:28,587
I hope it's built in time for her,

298
00:26:28,587 --> 00:26:32,341
but this model is
also really attractive.

299
00:26:32,341 --> 00:26:36,178
Isn't that too modern for us to live in?

300
00:26:36,178 --> 00:26:38,264
Innovative, like southern France.

301
00:26:38,264 --> 00:26:40,016
Sorry, we haven't...

302
00:26:40,016 --> 00:26:43,436
No problem, you can keep Plan B.

303
00:26:58,617 --> 00:27:01,620
Oh, a train.

304
00:27:02,455 --> 00:27:04,040
You drew it.

305
00:27:06,167 --> 00:27:07,793
Oh, right, not you.

306
00:27:08,669 --> 00:27:09,879
A train?

307
00:27:10,504 --> 00:27:14,216
Yeah, Kakeru liked trains.

308
00:27:15,926 --> 00:27:21,515
That's the Eno-den train line,
from there to there.

309
00:27:22,975 --> 00:27:24,602
Hmm...

310
00:27:35,529 --> 00:27:37,782
Before he even turned four,

311
00:27:38,407 --> 00:27:41,869
he memorized all the stops
on the Eno-den.

312
00:27:43,329 --> 00:27:45,706
-Me?
-Yup.

313
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:49,126
Kamakura,

314
00:27:51,003 --> 00:27:54,298
Wadazuka, Yuigahama,

315
00:27:56,008 --> 00:27:59,512
Hase, Gokurakuji,

316
00:27:59,512 --> 00:28:03,307
Inamuragasaki, Shichirigahama,

317
00:28:03,307 --> 00:28:05,643
Kamakura Koko-mae,

318
00:28:06,102 --> 00:28:08,229
-Koshigoe--
-Right.

319
00:28:08,646 --> 00:28:12,691
Enoshima, Shonan Kaigan Koen,

320
00:28:13,567 --> 00:28:17,488
Kugenuma, Yanagikoji,

321
00:28:17,488 --> 00:28:20,783
Ishigami, Fujisawa!

322
00:28:20,783 --> 00:28:22,743
Yay, yay!

323
00:28:37,133 --> 00:28:38,467
Gotta pee.

324
00:28:39,593 --> 00:28:40,845
Pee.

325
00:28:45,433 --> 00:28:49,979
A machine, a machine, a machine.

326
00:28:55,276 --> 00:28:57,445
A Roomba, a Roomba.

327
00:28:57,445 --> 00:28:59,155
A Roomba.

328
00:29:09,623 --> 00:29:10,916
Kakeru?

329
00:29:11,792 --> 00:29:14,503
-Where's Papa?
-He went out.

330
00:29:14,920 --> 00:29:16,881
-By himself?
-Yes.

331
00:29:17,339 --> 00:29:20,050
He should have taken you with him.

332
00:29:24,013 --> 00:29:26,432
What are you doing there?

333
00:29:26,432 --> 00:29:27,516
Shh!

334
00:29:58,672 --> 00:30:02,301
Kurihama, Kinugasa, Yokosuka,

335
00:30:02,301 --> 00:30:05,846
Taura, Higashi Zushi, Zushi,

336
00:30:05,846 --> 00:30:09,475
Kamakura, Kita Kamakura, Ofuna,

337
00:30:09,475 --> 00:30:13,103
Totsuka, Higashi Totsuka, Hodogaya,

338
00:30:13,103 --> 00:30:14,563
Yokohama...

339
00:30:20,194 --> 00:30:22,488
"I drew a sheep."

340
00:30:22,988 --> 00:30:26,534
"Say, this sheep looks sick."

341
00:30:27,243 --> 00:30:29,286
"I drew another sheep."

342
00:30:29,703 --> 00:30:33,374
"This sheep is a boy, he has horns."

343
00:30:33,374 --> 00:30:35,334
"Draw a girl sheep."

344
00:30:35,918 --> 00:30:38,087
"I drew a sheep again."

345
00:30:38,754 --> 00:30:43,467
"This sheep is a grandma.
Draw one that's younger."

346
00:30:44,510 --> 00:30:48,347
"I got sick of it and drew a box."

347
00:30:49,723 --> 00:30:53,686
"'Your sheep is in the box,' I said,

348
00:30:53,686 --> 00:30:55,479
and gave it to the child."

349
00:30:56,188 --> 00:30:59,775
"'Oh, this is the kind of sheep
I wanted,'

350
00:30:59,775 --> 00:31:01,610
said the child."

351
00:31:02,486 --> 00:31:03,779
"This sheep..."

352
00:31:08,075 --> 00:31:12,538
This isn't a sheep, it's a box, right?

353
00:31:13,414 --> 00:31:16,792
He says there's a sheep inside the box.

354
00:31:17,543 --> 00:31:20,462
How did he know that?

355
00:31:20,921 --> 00:31:25,968
How can he see what's inside
when he can't see it?

356
00:31:27,845 --> 00:31:29,555
I wonder why.

357
00:31:31,515 --> 00:31:34,727
Maybe it was crying like a sheep.

358
00:31:35,102 --> 00:31:37,313
It wasn't crying.

359
00:31:37,688 --> 00:31:40,983
"You can't see
the things that really matter."

360
00:31:40,983 --> 00:31:42,610
The fox says so.

361
00:31:43,444 --> 00:31:46,196
Just like when you build a house.

362
00:31:46,196 --> 00:31:49,700
What matters are the things you can't see.

363
00:31:52,578 --> 00:31:54,538
Shall we go to sleep?

364
00:31:54,955 --> 00:31:56,040
Yes.

365
00:32:05,382 --> 00:32:06,592
Good night.

366
00:32:07,426 --> 00:32:09,303
Good night.

367
00:32:10,721 --> 00:32:12,348
See you tomorrow.

368
00:32:19,521 --> 00:32:23,025
I think Kakeru
is more than just a machine.

369
00:32:24,026 --> 00:32:26,028
There's something here...

370
00:32:29,406 --> 00:32:31,659
Don't tell me he has a soul.

371
00:32:32,576 --> 00:32:36,038
Actually, why are we whispering?

372
00:32:37,581 --> 00:32:41,126
You're always saying trees have souls.

373
00:32:41,502 --> 00:32:44,088
That's different.

374
00:32:46,882 --> 00:32:51,845
Earlier, Kakeru was sitting out there
watching the trains.

375
00:32:54,848 --> 00:32:59,311
Then he started reciting the stops
on the Yokosuka line.

376
00:33:00,062 --> 00:33:02,731
I was so surprised.

377
00:33:06,527 --> 00:33:09,238
-It is strange.
-Right?

378
00:33:09,863 --> 00:33:12,866
As we both sat there together...

379
00:33:14,993 --> 00:33:16,495
I thought...

380
00:33:17,996 --> 00:33:20,833
actually, Kakeru has been alive...

381
00:33:25,379 --> 00:33:28,173
That was all a dream.

382
00:33:34,930 --> 00:33:36,640
Thanks for the food.

383
00:33:36,640 --> 00:33:40,018
Kakeru, Papa's going out.

384
00:33:40,018 --> 00:33:41,478
-Don't bother.
-Coming!

385
00:33:53,365 --> 00:33:55,033
-Off you go.
-Off you go.

386
00:33:57,077 --> 00:33:58,495
I'll be back.

387
00:34:06,587 --> 00:34:09,006
He never eats his onions.

388
00:34:13,761 --> 00:34:15,721
Oh, he forgot this.

389
00:34:16,263 --> 00:34:18,682
Oh, you're right.

390
00:34:20,684 --> 00:34:22,853
I'll take it to him.

391
00:34:23,270 --> 00:34:24,396
Wait.

392
00:34:25,981 --> 00:34:29,777
Let's deliver it to him later,
the two of us.

393
00:34:47,419 --> 00:34:50,631
Do you like Nishizawa Ryue?

394
00:34:51,006 --> 00:34:52,132
What?

395
00:34:53,217 --> 00:34:56,303
The curved lines of this glass,

396
00:34:56,303 --> 00:34:58,555
and how the boxes connect.

397
00:34:59,223 --> 00:35:03,811
Amazing you can see that.
He was my mentor.

398
00:35:04,686 --> 00:35:07,147
I read it in one of those books.

399
00:35:08,232 --> 00:35:12,820
I'm trying to find a good way
to combine glass and wood,

400
00:35:12,820 --> 00:35:15,531
two totally different materials.

401
00:35:17,991 --> 00:35:20,202
Sounds difficult.

402
00:35:20,536 --> 00:35:21,703
It is.

403
00:35:22,871 --> 00:35:26,667
That's why I love architecture so much.

404
00:35:28,418 --> 00:35:31,255
Are you planting a tree here?

405
00:35:31,255 --> 00:35:35,926
Yes, they saw your lemon tree yesterday
and want a tree.

406
00:35:36,510 --> 00:35:38,178
My lemon tree?

407
00:35:42,182 --> 00:35:45,143
We planted it when you were born.

408
00:35:46,436 --> 00:35:49,022
Was a tree planted when you were born?

409
00:35:49,356 --> 00:35:50,440
Yes.

410
00:35:51,024 --> 00:35:52,651
What kind of tree?

411
00:35:53,443 --> 00:35:55,153
What do you think?

412
00:35:57,030 --> 00:35:59,199
Where was it planted?

413
00:35:59,700 --> 00:36:01,577
A place called Hiroshima.

414
00:36:02,327 --> 00:36:04,913
In the entryway? The inner garden?

415
00:36:06,123 --> 00:36:07,457
The entryway.

416
00:36:08,041 --> 00:36:10,002
So...

417
00:36:11,169 --> 00:36:12,796
Crape myrtle?

418
00:36:13,463 --> 00:36:16,466
Amazing! How did you know?

419
00:36:17,050 --> 00:36:21,805
For an entryway,
it's either crape myrtle or pine.

420
00:36:22,389 --> 00:36:26,310
For a garden, plum or persimmon.

421
00:36:30,772 --> 00:36:33,108
Let me help you.

422
00:36:33,108 --> 00:36:35,027
I can do it myself.

423
00:36:49,291 --> 00:36:51,877
Okay, the other foot.

424
00:37:00,928 --> 00:37:03,722
You love the passenger seat.

425
00:37:03,722 --> 00:37:05,933
-Yeah.
-Why do you like it?

426
00:37:05,933 --> 00:37:10,395
I can see so many views,

427
00:37:10,395 --> 00:37:14,232
and how the trees and things
are growing.

428
00:37:14,900 --> 00:37:18,320
And how many times to turn,

429
00:37:19,112 --> 00:37:21,031
the steering wheel...

430
00:37:24,076 --> 00:37:25,160
Othello?

431
00:37:26,286 --> 00:37:29,122
-Who is it?
-One of us.

432
00:37:29,957 --> 00:37:31,249
One of us?

433
00:37:49,434 --> 00:37:53,522
How did you explain Kakeru to them?

434
00:37:53,522 --> 00:37:56,984
Nothing. I just told them the truth.

435
00:37:57,943 --> 00:37:59,444
Hmm.

436
00:38:02,322 --> 00:38:04,282
This is for Taka.

437
00:38:04,282 --> 00:38:08,412
She'll be happy.
She goes on maternity leave soon.

438
00:38:24,469 --> 00:38:25,595
Man, I was nervous.

439
00:38:26,596 --> 00:38:29,683
So sorry.
Suddenly says she's showing up.

440
00:38:29,683 --> 00:38:32,227
I have no idea how to act "normally."

441
00:38:32,227 --> 00:38:34,438
-Right?
-Right.

442
00:38:34,438 --> 00:38:36,189
Looks just like him.

443
00:38:36,189 --> 00:38:39,026
Boss, he must be super expensive.

444
00:38:39,026 --> 00:38:42,988
No, no. We were specially selected,
so he's free.

445
00:38:44,865 --> 00:38:47,325
-Promotional?
-Can I touch him?

446
00:38:47,325 --> 00:38:49,661
No, he's not a pet.

447
00:39:26,364 --> 00:39:28,533
Don't touch the tools.

448
00:39:30,077 --> 00:39:31,161
Okay.

449
00:39:54,101 --> 00:39:58,313
This is cypress. It came from Kiso.

450
00:39:59,231 --> 00:40:02,025
We carpenters call it Bishu.

451
00:40:04,361 --> 00:40:08,448
This one is about 300 years old.

452
00:40:10,367 --> 00:40:14,621
The mountains are cold,
so they grow slowly.

453
00:40:17,290 --> 00:40:18,583
Take a look.

454
00:40:19,751 --> 00:40:21,461
The grains are tightly packed.

455
00:40:22,963 --> 00:40:26,383
Which is what makes it sturdy.

456
00:40:27,092 --> 00:40:28,844
Just like humans.

457
00:40:30,011 --> 00:40:34,474
Where I come from, it's warmer,
so I'm not as sturdy...

458
00:40:38,019 --> 00:40:40,438
Did I tell you this story before?

459
00:40:43,024 --> 00:40:44,442
Okay.

460
00:40:50,365 --> 00:40:52,617
This is black pine.

461
00:40:55,162 --> 00:40:58,707
Listen to it, like this.

462
00:41:00,500 --> 00:41:02,669
It's tree time.

463
00:41:15,307 --> 00:41:17,017
Where's that from?

464
00:41:19,477 --> 00:41:22,230
The grandpa gave it to me.

465
00:41:22,230 --> 00:41:25,859
The man who's like an old tree.

466
00:41:25,859 --> 00:41:27,319
That's Akio.

467
00:41:28,486 --> 00:41:32,240
He said trees are alive
even after they die.

468
00:41:33,783 --> 00:41:36,203
He told me that too, years ago.

469
00:41:37,037 --> 00:41:41,124
"Honor the lifespan
of the tree you killed."

470
00:41:43,001 --> 00:41:45,420
Do you want more of these?

471
00:41:46,713 --> 00:41:47,839
Yeah.

472
00:41:48,590 --> 00:41:50,634
Oh, Kakeru.

473
00:41:51,509 --> 00:41:53,136
Building blocks.

474
00:41:57,599 --> 00:41:59,392
Amazing.

475
00:42:00,101 --> 00:42:02,646
-Yes, they're cute.
-Yeah.

476
00:42:03,355 --> 00:42:07,400
Let's put this here, with the bags.

477
00:42:07,400 --> 00:42:09,277
-Shall we get this one?
-Yeah.

478
00:42:09,736 --> 00:42:11,404
Let's get this one.

479
00:42:16,826 --> 00:42:18,495
A xylophone.

480
00:42:21,331 --> 00:42:24,417
Oh, Kakeru, Kakeru.

481
00:42:26,127 --> 00:42:28,338
Look, The Little Prince.

482
00:42:44,938 --> 00:42:48,275
In the eye of the storm,
maximum wind speeds...

483
00:42:48,275 --> 00:42:50,277
I'm glad the typhoon missed us.

484
00:42:53,280 --> 00:42:56,324
Kakeru, what are you making?

485
00:42:56,741 --> 00:42:58,118
A house.

486
00:42:58,118 --> 00:43:00,495
Wow, I can't wait.

487
00:43:02,664 --> 00:43:04,666
That's domestic pine, right?

488
00:43:06,334 --> 00:43:08,753
Pine is for beams, not pillars.

489
00:43:10,672 --> 00:43:12,090
Across.

490
00:43:12,090 --> 00:43:13,383
That's right.

491
00:43:14,259 --> 00:43:16,094
It's called a taiko beam.

492
00:43:16,094 --> 00:43:20,640
Beams that are curved like this
are strong and lasting,

493
00:43:20,640 --> 00:43:23,476
and used to symbolize the house.

494
00:43:24,102 --> 00:43:28,064
But now they use imported pine
or laminated wood.

495
00:43:28,648 --> 00:43:31,192
Kakeru can't understand all that.

496
00:43:31,192 --> 00:43:34,529
The boy went missing last month,

497
00:43:34,529 --> 00:43:36,906
after he was last seen by his friend.

498
00:43:37,490 --> 00:43:43,246
He was wearing an orange and white
striped T-shirt and shorts.

499
00:43:43,246 --> 00:43:47,709
Other missing persons include
a seven year-old in Fujisawa...

500
00:43:47,709 --> 00:43:52,297
I think he's the same kidnapper as when...

501
00:43:56,009 --> 00:43:56,885
I wonder...

502
00:44:01,139 --> 00:44:03,433
-How about the slide?
-Sure.

503
00:44:05,268 --> 00:44:07,687
-Don't fall.
-Sure.

504
00:44:54,025 --> 00:44:55,735
Who was that boy?

505
00:45:18,508 --> 00:45:20,468
What are you doing?
Get out of the way!

506
00:45:20,468 --> 00:45:22,762
Hey! Can you hear me?

507
00:45:27,684 --> 00:45:29,936
It's heavy, hurry up!

508
00:45:29,936 --> 00:45:31,187
Kakeru!

509
00:45:32,230 --> 00:45:34,274
Kakeru. Kakeru?

510
00:45:43,158 --> 00:45:44,117
Kakeru.

511
00:45:44,117 --> 00:45:46,703
Kakeru, Kakeru.

512
00:45:46,703 --> 00:45:47,954
Kakeru!

513
00:45:48,496 --> 00:45:50,248
Kakeru! Kakeru!

514
00:45:50,248 --> 00:45:51,833
Does he need water?

515
00:45:51,833 --> 00:45:53,001
An ambulance...

516
00:45:56,379 --> 00:46:00,592
Oh, thank you. We're okay.

517
00:46:19,861 --> 00:46:21,029
Don't worry.

518
00:46:21,863 --> 00:46:23,656
I won't die.

519
00:46:27,368 --> 00:46:28,828
Thank goodness.

520
00:46:30,497 --> 00:46:33,124
I forgot and left you too far away.

521
00:46:33,917 --> 00:46:36,628
I'm sorry. Are you feeling okay?

522
00:46:36,961 --> 00:46:37,879
Yeah.

523
00:46:39,255 --> 00:46:42,091
-Want to try the slide again?
-Sure.

524
00:46:50,934 --> 00:46:53,436
Maybe this is a beam.

525
00:48:01,838 --> 00:48:04,132
This is hot, be careful.

526
00:48:04,132 --> 00:48:05,133
Yeah.

527
00:48:14,559 --> 00:48:18,354
Don't you need a pillar here?

528
00:48:19,022 --> 00:48:23,359
I'll use laminated kara -pine here,
so it's fine.

529
00:48:23,818 --> 00:48:27,071
That way, the pillar
won't block the view.

530
00:48:27,905 --> 00:48:29,949
If the beam is made of solid wood,

531
00:48:29,949 --> 00:48:31,868
does it have to have a pillar?

532
00:48:32,285 --> 00:48:33,786
That's right.

533
00:48:33,786 --> 00:48:35,580
Should I use solid wood?

534
00:48:36,497 --> 00:48:38,875
Smells better, right?

535
00:48:39,667 --> 00:48:41,294
You sound like your papa.

536
00:48:42,170 --> 00:48:44,589
I can't tell the difference.

537
00:48:46,215 --> 00:48:47,508
Where are you going?

538
00:48:49,093 --> 00:48:50,595
To my room.

539
00:48:51,929 --> 00:48:53,598
I'm making a house.

540
00:48:54,432 --> 00:48:55,725
What kind?

541
00:48:56,809 --> 00:48:58,227
Hmm.

542
00:48:59,228 --> 00:49:00,813
A secret?

543
00:49:00,813 --> 00:49:01,856
Yeah.

544
00:49:02,398 --> 00:49:06,277
So don't come in until I'm finished, okay?

545
00:49:07,403 --> 00:49:08,571
Got it.

546
00:49:09,864 --> 00:49:11,324
Promise.

547
00:49:29,342 --> 00:49:30,551
Breathing?

548
00:49:30,551 --> 00:49:31,552
Yeah.

549
00:49:31,552 --> 00:49:34,222
It's still breathing.

550
00:49:34,222 --> 00:49:36,683
That's proof of life.

551
00:49:37,558 --> 00:49:38,893
I see.

552
00:49:40,269 --> 00:49:44,899
We don't breathe, right?
Does that mean we're dead?

553
00:49:45,525 --> 00:49:50,071
We absorb a type of energy
and emit that as heat,

554
00:49:50,071 --> 00:49:52,448
so that's a kind of breathing.

555
00:49:52,907 --> 00:49:54,242
Raise your left arm.

556
00:49:54,951 --> 00:49:57,870
Does that mean we're alive?

557
00:49:57,870 --> 00:50:00,540
We are definitely born from death.

558
00:50:00,540 --> 00:50:03,084
But humans were once inorganic, too.

559
00:50:03,084 --> 00:50:07,505
We just evolved into organic matter
faster than they did.

560
00:50:09,048 --> 00:50:11,217
Here, the GPS.

561
00:50:14,262 --> 00:50:16,806
Now you can go as far as you want.

562
00:50:20,643 --> 00:50:23,062
Your body belongs to you, now.

563
00:50:25,148 --> 00:50:28,359
How come you know all this stuff?

564
00:50:29,736 --> 00:50:31,279
They taught me.

565
00:50:31,279 --> 00:50:34,073
Who did? A human?

566
00:50:34,741 --> 00:50:37,785
Yeah, someone you don't know.

567
00:50:42,415 --> 00:50:47,378
Said, "You're not the humans' past,
but their future."

568
00:50:48,796 --> 00:50:55,344
DO NOT ENTER

569
00:50:56,763 --> 00:50:58,264
Their future...

570
00:51:10,276 --> 00:51:11,444
Mother.

571
00:51:11,444 --> 00:51:15,072
It's so hot, even now, in October.

572
00:51:15,072 --> 00:51:18,367
-You didn't tell me...
-I thought I called.

573
00:51:18,367 --> 00:51:20,536
It's a bit of a mess.

574
00:51:20,536 --> 00:51:23,956
This is for Acorn,
your husband, his favorite.

575
00:51:23,956 --> 00:51:26,209
His nickname's Chestnut Burs.

576
00:51:26,209 --> 00:51:27,460
Oh, yeah?

577
00:51:27,460 --> 00:51:30,129
-I'm coming in!
-Coming in.

578
00:51:30,129 --> 00:51:32,882
You can't show up without notice.

579
00:51:32,882 --> 00:51:34,342
I won't stay long.

580
00:51:34,342 --> 00:51:37,637
I just need to catch the 4:00 p.m. train

581
00:51:37,637 --> 00:51:39,180
at Shin-Yokohama.

582
00:51:39,180 --> 00:51:40,890
Just stopping by on our way.

583
00:51:40,890 --> 00:51:42,350
Mama, someone's in there.

584
00:51:42,350 --> 00:51:43,184
Who?

585
00:51:44,060 --> 00:51:46,395
Kake...ru?

586
00:51:50,024 --> 00:51:51,567
Kakeru?

587
00:52:12,505 --> 00:52:15,883
Do your eyes shine?

588
00:52:16,217 --> 00:52:17,635
Kenta.

589
00:52:19,762 --> 00:52:24,392
My eyes don't shine,
but this does, here.

590
00:52:28,396 --> 00:52:31,858
Wow, look, Brother.

591
00:52:35,486 --> 00:52:36,779
You're right.

592
00:52:38,823 --> 00:52:40,283
Can I touch it?

593
00:52:45,204 --> 00:52:46,956
What's this?

594
00:52:49,000 --> 00:52:52,128
Can I go to the park with Kakeru?

595
00:52:53,004 --> 00:52:56,215
I'm sorry, Kakeru can't go very far.

596
00:52:56,591 --> 00:52:59,468
-Why not?
-Because...

597
00:52:59,927 --> 00:53:01,178
Mama.

598
00:53:02,138 --> 00:53:05,308
Kakeru has a button here.

599
00:53:05,308 --> 00:53:07,685
-Can I push it?
-No.

600
00:53:09,604 --> 00:53:13,274
What in the...

601
00:53:13,691 --> 00:53:16,360
There are 3,000 across Japan.

602
00:53:16,360 --> 00:53:19,780
You're still young, have another child.

603
00:53:19,780 --> 00:53:21,157
Easy to say.

604
00:53:21,157 --> 00:53:23,242
Ouch, hope it's not cracked.

605
00:53:23,242 --> 00:53:24,744
Don't get wet!

606
00:53:24,744 --> 00:53:27,705
Don't worry, you have tough bones.

607
00:53:29,999 --> 00:53:32,293
He's sure well made.

608
00:53:32,752 --> 00:53:34,045
Don't say that.

609
00:53:34,045 --> 00:53:36,672
He's so perfectly made.

610
00:53:36,672 --> 00:53:39,008
Don't you find him creepy?

611
00:53:39,008 --> 00:53:42,762
-No clean clothes!
-Not if you live with him.

612
00:53:42,762 --> 00:53:44,931
He's just like a Roomba.

613
00:53:44,931 --> 00:53:46,098
Ouch!

614
00:53:54,065 --> 00:53:57,109
What are you supposed to do now?

615
00:53:57,902 --> 00:54:01,614
I did it because Kakeru did it first.

616
00:54:01,614 --> 00:54:04,408
Go tell him you're sorry.

617
00:54:05,117 --> 00:54:07,495
But he did it first.

618
00:54:08,245 --> 00:54:10,998
Everything okay with you?

619
00:54:11,582 --> 00:54:13,751
Yeah, I'm fine.

620
00:54:15,086 --> 00:54:17,546
-How many fingers?
-One.

621
00:54:18,172 --> 00:54:19,382
Two.

622
00:54:25,137 --> 00:54:26,430
Do it.

623
00:54:30,059 --> 00:54:32,019
I'm sorry.

624
00:54:35,189 --> 00:54:36,565
You, too, Kakeru.

625
00:54:40,987 --> 00:54:43,239
I'm sorry.

626
00:54:56,293 --> 00:55:00,589
Why do I have to apologize, too?

627
00:55:01,007 --> 00:55:03,592
-Let's play Concentration.
-Yeah.

628
00:55:03,926 --> 00:55:05,219
Because...

629
00:55:05,219 --> 00:55:08,097
I'm good at it, so I'll win.

630
00:55:14,729 --> 00:55:15,980
Are you okay?

631
00:55:18,691 --> 00:55:20,693
Tea's lukewarm now.

632
00:55:21,318 --> 00:55:22,653
I'll make another pot.

633
00:55:22,653 --> 00:55:25,614
Forget it, it's fine, what a waste.

634
00:55:25,614 --> 00:55:28,242
What time is your train?

635
00:55:28,242 --> 00:55:30,369
I'll never make it.

636
00:55:30,369 --> 00:55:32,913
You're staying the night?

637
00:55:32,913 --> 00:55:35,791
Hiroshima's far. I'll go tomorrow.

638
00:55:35,791 --> 00:55:38,169
I'm leaving. No spare clothes.

639
00:55:38,169 --> 00:55:42,465
Borrow hers.
You're about the same height.

640
00:55:42,465 --> 00:55:47,845
Departing now, you'll reach
Kita Kamakura station at 15:10,

641
00:55:47,845 --> 00:55:50,806
catch the 15:19 JR Yokosuka line.

642
00:55:50,806 --> 00:55:53,476
You the stationmaster?

643
00:55:53,476 --> 00:55:54,810
No.

644
00:55:54,810 --> 00:55:59,190
Thank you, but I'm staying. I'm tired.

645
00:55:59,607 --> 00:56:04,320
Ari, tomorrow let's go
to that soba restaurant, with...

646
00:56:04,320 --> 00:56:05,154
Matsubara-an...

647
00:56:05,529 --> 00:56:07,490
My Mama doesn't want you to stay over.

648
00:56:07,490 --> 00:56:08,407
Kakeru!

649
00:56:09,241 --> 00:56:11,285
What's going on here?

650
00:56:11,952 --> 00:56:13,746
Are you making him?

651
00:56:13,746 --> 00:56:15,331
Of course not.

652
00:56:15,331 --> 00:56:20,294
I gave you money
when you built this house.

653
00:56:20,294 --> 00:56:22,588
That was Dad.

654
00:56:22,588 --> 00:56:26,634
Humans don't say everything
they're thinking.

655
00:56:26,634 --> 00:56:28,052
But you do.

656
00:56:28,052 --> 00:56:30,471
My money comes with opinions.

657
00:56:30,471 --> 00:56:32,807
Then, I'll spend the night, too.

658
00:56:32,807 --> 00:56:36,894
Sister, I need pants. Mine are too tight.

659
00:56:36,894 --> 00:56:38,020
Sorry.

660
00:56:40,022 --> 00:56:41,857
So, sun-dried?

661
00:56:41,857 --> 00:56:43,984
Boiler-dried aren't good enough?

662
00:56:43,984 --> 00:56:45,027
No.

663
00:56:45,027 --> 00:56:48,572
He lines up chips in our yard.

664
00:56:49,198 --> 00:56:51,534
He inspects the grains,

665
00:56:51,534 --> 00:56:55,496
to decide if it should be a beam
or a door frame.

666
00:56:57,081 --> 00:56:58,749
Kakeru's an expert.

667
00:56:59,458 --> 00:57:04,255
We should take him
to Kiso or the Yoshino area.

668
00:57:04,255 --> 00:57:07,216
Excuse me, this isn't right.

669
00:57:07,216 --> 00:57:10,386
I've been asking you
to take me for a long time.

670
00:57:10,386 --> 00:57:13,806
But I'm always told,
"Not your time yet."

671
00:57:13,806 --> 00:57:17,560
Too bad. He's got a natural talent.

672
00:57:17,560 --> 00:57:19,061
Same for baseball.

673
00:57:22,064 --> 00:57:24,650
-65,000!
-I hear 65,000!

674
00:57:24,650 --> 00:57:26,110
-68,000!
-68,000.

675
00:57:26,110 --> 00:57:27,862
-70,000!
-70,000.

676
00:57:28,237 --> 00:57:30,865
Hey, wait, wait!

677
00:57:32,324 --> 00:57:35,035
Look, this grain is good.

678
00:57:35,035 --> 00:57:36,912
It's from Tochigi.

679
00:57:39,206 --> 00:57:42,001
Keep bidding higher, raise it.

680
00:57:43,961 --> 00:57:46,839
-Apple.
-There's an apple somewhere.

681
00:57:46,839 --> 00:57:48,632
Is this it? Nope, wrong!

682
00:57:48,632 --> 00:57:50,301
Where was it?

683
00:57:51,051 --> 00:57:52,094
Here!

684
00:57:54,722 --> 00:57:57,975
-A cat? Was there one before?
-Amazing!

685
00:57:59,560 --> 00:58:03,480
She hasn't made any progress at all.

686
00:58:05,858 --> 00:58:08,068
Grandma, you play, too!

687
00:58:08,068 --> 00:58:11,822
Grandma's good at that. I'll win, okay?

688
00:58:11,822 --> 00:58:13,157
No!

689
00:58:13,490 --> 00:58:17,912
Don't you want to see Ari, your favorite?

690
00:58:18,579 --> 00:58:21,207
One hour and I've had it.

691
00:58:24,877 --> 00:58:28,130
Cooking like it's her kitchen.

692
00:58:29,340 --> 00:58:31,300
How should I know?

693
00:58:31,300 --> 00:58:32,801
Here she is.

694
00:58:47,483 --> 00:58:48,692
This thing.

695
00:58:52,488 --> 00:58:53,614
Yes...

696
00:58:53,614 --> 00:58:56,575
Chop this damned tree down.

697
00:58:57,326 --> 00:58:59,620
-What?
-Cut it down.

698
00:59:00,454 --> 00:59:02,748
How dare you? This tree is...

699
00:59:02,748 --> 00:59:05,584
It just reminds you of him every day.

700
00:59:06,752 --> 00:59:08,003
That's fine.

701
00:59:08,003 --> 00:59:10,381
This is exactly why

702
00:59:10,756 --> 00:59:13,759
you can't move forward.

703
00:59:13,759 --> 00:59:15,636
So, I should forget him?

704
00:59:16,637 --> 00:59:20,516
Griefwork has, you know, stairs.

705
00:59:20,516 --> 00:59:23,394
There are stages. Six of them, I think.

706
00:59:23,394 --> 00:59:27,398
First, you deny they're dead.

707
00:59:28,148 --> 00:59:29,316
Blame yourself...

708
00:59:29,316 --> 00:59:31,443
You're reciting some book.

709
00:59:31,443 --> 00:59:33,862
Yes, I read it when your dad died.

710
00:59:34,405 --> 00:59:37,157
Send that thing back now.

711
00:59:37,157 --> 00:59:39,451
Not your business.

712
00:59:39,451 --> 00:59:45,249
It is my business.
I'm ashamed my daughter's doing this.

713
00:59:47,251 --> 00:59:51,005
I keep telling you, stop being my mother.

714
00:59:54,633 --> 00:59:58,721
Each time you said
you'd stop being my mother,

715
00:59:59,430 --> 01:00:02,308
the pain and fear kept me awake.

716
01:00:03,851 --> 01:00:07,771
I said I'm sorry,
crying and begging you not to.

717
01:00:09,189 --> 01:00:10,691
Now, it's the reverse.

718
01:00:11,734 --> 01:00:13,777
Stop showing up acting like my mother!

719
01:00:14,236 --> 01:00:17,906
I act like your mother
because I'm your mother.

720
01:00:23,454 --> 01:00:25,289
That day was the same.

721
01:00:29,001 --> 01:00:31,253
You showed up out of the blue.

722
01:00:32,838 --> 01:00:36,800
If you hadn't been here,
I would have picked him up.

723
01:00:37,468 --> 01:00:39,428
And Kakeru would never...

724
01:00:41,180 --> 01:00:42,931
have been...

725
01:00:49,480 --> 01:00:52,024
The squash will get overcooked.

726
01:01:53,168 --> 01:01:54,628
Where are you going?

727
01:01:58,173 --> 01:02:03,095
You can't follow strangers anymore.
Got that?

728
01:02:05,264 --> 01:02:06,390
Yeah.

729
01:02:57,065 --> 01:02:58,442
Othello?

730
01:03:01,320 --> 01:03:02,988
-Welcome home.
-I'm home.

731
01:03:04,072 --> 01:03:06,033
Kakeru, we're going.

732
01:03:10,287 --> 01:03:12,956
Want to play hide-and-seek? Yes.

733
01:03:16,793 --> 01:03:18,837
I'm the only one that raised my hand.

734
01:03:18,837 --> 01:03:19,713
Sad.

735
01:03:26,762 --> 01:03:29,181
It's okay. Come here.

736
01:03:29,181 --> 01:03:30,516
Who is that kid?

737
01:03:32,142 --> 01:03:35,604
Tetta. I'm going to shelter him here.

738
01:03:37,523 --> 01:03:39,316
Is your leg okay?

739
01:03:40,234 --> 01:03:43,153
My father did it to me.

740
01:03:44,071 --> 01:03:46,823
His father will kill him.

741
01:03:47,407 --> 01:03:48,909
But he's human, right?

742
01:03:48,909 --> 01:03:52,538
-I'm human, too.
-I'm glad I have human company.

743
01:03:52,538 --> 01:03:54,581
Will he get in our way?

744
01:03:54,581 --> 01:03:58,669
Well, humans have their role to play.

745
01:03:58,669 --> 01:04:01,505
Ume found this abandoned school.

746
01:04:01,505 --> 01:04:02,839
Right?

747
01:04:03,840 --> 01:04:06,093
I'll treat your leg.

748
01:04:07,386 --> 01:04:09,888
-Thanks.
-Let's play hide-and-seek.

749
01:04:09,888 --> 01:04:12,057
-What's your name?
-Mitsuki.

750
01:04:12,057 --> 01:04:14,351
Ten, nine...

751
01:04:14,351 --> 01:04:15,602
Kakeru.

752
01:04:21,900 --> 01:04:24,778
This place isn't really safe for us.

753
01:04:26,488 --> 01:04:29,491
Can you find a place
we can all live together?

754
01:04:29,491 --> 01:04:30,784
Yeah.

755
01:04:30,784 --> 01:04:33,245
Ready or not!

756
01:04:39,835 --> 01:04:45,173
When a tree dies,
fungi break it down into soil

757
01:04:45,632 --> 01:04:49,428
and a new tree rises in its place.

758
01:04:49,428 --> 01:04:53,890
That circulatory system
is similar to ours.

759
01:04:57,227 --> 01:05:02,065
Normally trees are connected
to each other through chemicals,

760
01:05:02,065 --> 01:05:05,068
that roots and leaves send out.

761
01:05:05,402 --> 01:05:08,947
But woodlands are disappearing...

762
01:05:17,664 --> 01:05:20,959
Hello, I'm on site at the plot of land.

763
01:05:28,467 --> 01:05:31,386
I'll remake the model then.

764
01:05:33,096 --> 01:05:36,892
No, it's totally fine.
Please don't worry.

765
01:05:39,353 --> 01:05:40,437
Yes.

766
01:05:45,317 --> 01:05:49,154
His mother's saying
she won't live with them.

767
01:05:50,030 --> 01:05:52,032
She didn't like the house?

768
01:05:52,491 --> 01:05:57,204
No, she wants to live with her lover.

769
01:05:58,413 --> 01:06:00,207
Good for her.

770
01:06:01,083 --> 01:06:05,462
Without the tatami room,
the floor plan will change.

771
01:06:08,715 --> 01:06:13,679
TO OTO: FREE RANGE EGGS ONLY!
NO WHITE SUGAR!

772
01:06:13,679 --> 01:06:18,600
I WANT TO HOLD MY GRANDCHILD.
A REAL ONE! YOUR MOTHER

773
01:06:32,781 --> 01:06:37,703
How about making the tatami room
the children's room?

774
01:06:40,163 --> 01:06:44,543
The balance is off.
So is the staggered layout.

775
01:06:44,543 --> 01:06:48,630
Separating the children's
and parents' rooms loses...

776
01:06:48,630 --> 01:06:53,051
Don't touch that.
It's important for my work.

777
01:06:54,261 --> 01:06:56,680
You're still using it?

778
01:06:56,680 --> 01:07:00,267
Why don't you start over again...

779
01:07:04,730 --> 01:07:08,483
It's an earlier model
that I want to save.

780
01:07:09,901 --> 01:07:11,236
Why?

781
01:07:13,363 --> 01:07:16,533
I don't look for quick solutions.

782
01:07:16,533 --> 01:07:18,702
But you won't understand.

783
01:07:19,536 --> 01:07:24,458
I want to struggle
with lots of different ideas.

784
01:07:24,958 --> 01:07:26,710
You want to struggle?

785
01:07:27,085 --> 01:07:30,338
That's right. I enjoy it.

786
01:07:30,338 --> 01:07:32,591
That's the nature of life.

787
01:07:33,008 --> 01:07:34,676
Of life...

788
01:07:52,819 --> 01:07:55,822
Are you happier without me here?

789
01:07:57,991 --> 01:07:59,159
What?

790
01:08:03,789 --> 01:08:06,166
Who said that?

791
01:08:18,720 --> 01:08:21,848
Stop staring inside me.

792
01:08:25,101 --> 01:08:26,311
I'm sorry.

793
01:08:27,103 --> 01:08:29,523
Kakeru never did that.

794
01:08:30,273 --> 01:08:33,819
No child of mine
would do something like that.

795
01:08:45,872 --> 01:08:48,917
Does that mean
you're sending me back?

796
01:08:52,420 --> 01:08:53,505
What?

797
01:08:54,297 --> 01:08:57,133
Will you terminate the contract?

798
01:09:00,595 --> 01:09:03,807
I need... to think about it.

799
01:09:09,479 --> 01:09:11,314
Will you terminate...

800
01:09:11,314 --> 01:09:15,443
Yes, I will. I'll stop being your mother.

801
01:09:17,153 --> 01:09:18,738
I understand.

802
01:09:32,419 --> 01:09:35,171
Kurihama, Kinugasa,

803
01:09:35,171 --> 01:09:39,718
Yokosuka, Taura, Higashi Zushi...

804
01:09:52,439 --> 01:09:53,648
Kakeru!

805
01:10:00,989 --> 01:10:06,494
The fingertips and feet on this model
are a little weak.

806
01:10:06,494 --> 01:10:08,580
They often stumble.

807
01:10:09,998 --> 01:10:13,919
Don't worry, I'm a human being.

808
01:10:18,089 --> 01:10:19,883
Its fluid is leaking.

809
01:10:23,470 --> 01:10:25,889
Did it bump into something?

810
01:10:32,812 --> 01:10:35,732
It's fine, it doesn't feel any pain.

811
01:10:37,192 --> 01:10:40,987
Don't worry, it can't sense pain
or smell anything.

812
01:10:49,204 --> 01:10:51,039
Don't touch it.

813
01:10:56,169 --> 01:11:00,090
How about suffering or sadness?

814
01:11:01,591 --> 01:11:04,928
It doesn't have a soul.

815
01:11:04,928 --> 01:11:06,805
If it looks like it does...

816
01:11:07,889 --> 01:11:11,226
it's just reflecting your emotions.

817
01:11:16,898 --> 01:11:18,608
My emotions...

818
01:11:27,158 --> 01:11:31,621
Essentially, it's no more
than a product of your own past.

819
01:11:45,760 --> 01:11:47,679
Any other wounds?

820
01:12:31,765 --> 01:12:33,433
You get it?

821
01:12:34,267 --> 01:12:36,811
-I felt the same way.
-Really?

822
01:12:36,811 --> 01:12:39,147
I was so moved...

823
01:12:50,950 --> 01:12:54,996
Babies should hear
lots of English from age zero.

824
01:12:54,996 --> 01:12:55,997
Age zero?

825
01:13:18,686 --> 01:13:21,356
MISSING

826
01:13:21,356 --> 01:13:24,818
Mama doesn't get along with her mama.

827
01:13:25,568 --> 01:13:26,736
Why not?

828
01:13:27,403 --> 01:13:29,072
Because they're alike.

829
01:13:29,072 --> 01:13:30,824
Hmm.

830
01:13:32,075 --> 01:13:33,701
Aren't they alike?

831
01:13:35,286 --> 01:13:38,665
Huge. This octopus looks tasty.

832
01:13:39,791 --> 01:13:45,171
Cook it till it's tender
and wash it down with a beer.

833
01:13:45,171 --> 01:13:46,840
After my bath.

834
01:13:49,050 --> 01:13:51,469
Not that you'd understand.

835
01:13:52,595 --> 01:13:53,888
Hmm.

836
01:13:53,888 --> 01:13:55,098
Oh!

837
01:13:59,686 --> 01:14:02,480
I didn't mean it like that.

838
01:14:04,107 --> 01:14:09,571
I mean, a child can't appreciate
a beer after a bath.

839
01:14:09,571 --> 01:14:11,447
-Well...
-Right?

840
01:14:13,783 --> 01:14:15,076
Don't be mad.

841
01:14:16,536 --> 01:14:19,455
Jellyfish are also edible.

842
01:14:19,455 --> 01:14:23,126
Did Kakeru take baths?

843
01:14:23,543 --> 01:14:24,502
Kakeru?

844
01:14:25,336 --> 01:14:27,297
He took baths with me.

845
01:14:28,882 --> 01:14:32,719
But he didn't like them. Got out quick.

846
01:14:34,179 --> 01:14:38,057
Couldn't even stay
30 seconds in the water.

847
01:14:49,068 --> 01:14:51,237
That sardine looks tasty, too.

848
01:14:51,905 --> 01:14:55,742
Cooked with pickled plums.
You can eat the bones.

849
01:14:56,409 --> 01:14:59,120
This isn't a fish store.

850
01:15:00,997 --> 01:15:03,958
Mama said the same thing to me before.

851
01:15:06,085 --> 01:15:07,128
What?

852
01:15:13,801 --> 01:15:16,054
Oh, Mr. Komoto.

853
01:15:17,472 --> 01:15:19,599
Are you Kakeru?

854
01:15:21,267 --> 01:15:25,188
Are... Are you Airu?

855
01:15:25,188 --> 01:15:26,773
You remember him?

856
01:15:44,624 --> 01:15:47,585
This is where some man said to you...

857
01:15:51,256 --> 01:15:53,258
"Let's go see the train."

858
01:15:57,470 --> 01:15:59,389
Do you remember anything?

859
01:16:05,853 --> 01:16:07,272
Got it.

860
01:16:11,359 --> 01:16:12,902
Next stop, then.

861
01:17:18,051 --> 01:17:21,763
So this is where I died.

862
01:17:22,764 --> 01:17:26,893
That's right!
Can you remember anything?

863
01:17:27,810 --> 01:17:30,271
The color of the car...

864
01:17:31,272 --> 01:17:34,609
the man's face, anything?

865
01:17:38,738 --> 01:17:41,032
You remembered Airu.

866
01:17:42,241 --> 01:17:46,454
That's because Airu
was in kindergarten with me.

867
01:17:53,419 --> 01:17:56,047
We sent them your group photo.

868
01:17:57,465 --> 01:17:59,300
From graduation.

869
01:18:03,221 --> 01:18:04,931
That's right.

870
01:18:07,350 --> 01:18:08,851
That's it.

871
01:18:14,941 --> 01:18:17,193
I was supposed to take you.

872
01:18:21,239 --> 01:18:25,451
I was supposed to pick you up.
Mama had asked me.

873
01:18:30,665 --> 01:18:32,250
But I was late.

874
01:18:34,794 --> 01:18:36,087
And then...

875
01:18:38,089 --> 01:18:40,425
You couldn't wait anymore.

876
01:18:44,554 --> 01:18:46,556
Why were you late?

877
01:18:50,017 --> 01:18:52,103
I was playing Pachinko.

878
01:18:58,067 --> 01:19:00,069
And I hit the jackpot.

879
01:19:08,536 --> 01:19:09,954
I'm sorry.

880
01:19:14,542 --> 01:19:16,210
I'm sorry.

881
01:19:18,796 --> 01:19:20,214
I'm so sorry.

882
01:19:20,923 --> 01:19:22,341
It's okay.

883
01:19:30,433 --> 01:19:32,768
I have to find who did it.

884
01:19:35,104 --> 01:19:37,148
It's not anybody's fault.

885
01:19:39,984 --> 01:19:41,319
Please...

886
01:19:42,737 --> 01:19:45,406
Kakeru, please...

887
01:19:47,992 --> 01:19:50,203
What are you doing here!

888
01:19:52,997 --> 01:19:55,917
What... Nothing, let's go.

889
01:19:57,126 --> 01:20:00,421
We had a report of someone suspicious
walking with a child.

890
01:20:00,421 --> 01:20:03,090
Looking for our cat.

891
01:20:04,050 --> 01:20:07,345
-He's my son.
-Is he your father?

892
01:20:07,345 --> 01:20:09,514
-I'm Papa, right?
-Shut up.

893
01:20:09,514 --> 01:20:10,640
The hell!

894
01:20:12,058 --> 01:20:13,559
-Stop it!
-Hey!

895
01:20:14,560 --> 01:20:15,770
Hey!

896
01:20:15,770 --> 01:20:18,439
-Calm down.
-Let me go!

897
01:20:18,439 --> 01:20:21,734
Is this man your father?

898
01:20:24,779 --> 01:20:26,364
Mister...

899
01:20:26,364 --> 01:20:27,532
Hey!

900
01:20:27,532 --> 01:20:29,242
You, wait!

901
01:20:39,585 --> 01:20:43,005
I should have called him Papa.

902
01:20:43,005 --> 01:20:47,552
That's right.
We thought he was trying to kidnap you.

903
01:20:47,552 --> 01:20:49,220
He didn't kidnap me.

904
01:20:51,973 --> 01:20:57,770
We investigated the case
of Komoto Kakeru as a crime and accident.

905
01:20:57,770 --> 01:20:59,855
We ruled it an accident.

906
01:20:59,855 --> 01:21:01,899
I know all about that!

907
01:21:02,817 --> 01:21:05,570
First of all, you up there!

908
01:21:06,404 --> 01:21:09,490
You called me a suspicious person!

909
01:21:11,033 --> 01:21:15,746
Two years ago, you treated us both
as suspects, too.

910
01:21:15,746 --> 01:21:18,165
-No, we did not.
-Yes, you did.

911
01:21:18,624 --> 01:21:20,001
I'm sorry.

912
01:21:20,001 --> 01:21:22,128
Leave me out of it.

913
01:21:22,712 --> 01:21:24,714
Arrest the perpetrator.

914
01:21:26,340 --> 01:21:29,010
How many kids have gone missing?

915
01:21:45,151 --> 01:21:46,777
Calling you mister...

916
01:21:50,781 --> 01:21:52,116
Don't tell me...

917
01:21:53,200 --> 01:21:56,454
this is what you had in mind all along.

918
01:21:57,288 --> 01:21:59,123
Of course not.

919
01:22:00,041 --> 01:22:01,667
Taking him there,

920
01:22:02,460 --> 01:22:05,463
dressed in Kakeru's old clothes.

921
01:22:06,213 --> 01:22:08,633
Where did you get that idea?

922
01:22:09,258 --> 01:22:12,595
I want to catch the perpetrator...

923
01:22:12,595 --> 01:22:16,724
There is no perpetrator, anywhere.

924
01:22:18,100 --> 01:22:21,979
You just want to believe

925
01:22:21,979 --> 01:22:25,191
there's a perpetrator,
that a bad guy killed him.

926
01:22:26,317 --> 01:22:30,988
You're running away.
To believe we're not responsible.

927
01:22:30,988 --> 01:22:33,616
I have to run away!

928
01:22:41,290 --> 01:22:43,000
You, too...

929
01:22:44,335 --> 01:22:45,586
What?

930
01:22:47,463 --> 01:22:49,215
You, too, what?

931
01:22:51,509 --> 01:22:55,554
You've always blamed it
on your mother, too.

932
01:23:01,477 --> 01:23:04,146
What about Kakeru's stomach?

933
01:23:07,566 --> 01:23:08,818
What?

934
01:23:08,818 --> 01:23:13,406
His wound here, when I dressed him.

935
01:23:16,033 --> 01:23:19,078
-He fell, from there.
-Why?

936
01:23:19,078 --> 01:23:21,622
I don't know! I don't know!

937
01:23:21,622 --> 01:23:25,042
-Why didn't you tell me?
-I don't know!

938
01:23:33,509 --> 01:23:34,927
It's my fault.

939
01:23:37,304 --> 01:23:41,642
It's because I said that to him,
that Kakeru...

940
01:23:45,020 --> 01:23:47,148
-Because I...
-Send him back?

941
01:23:52,737 --> 01:23:54,071
We should.

942
01:24:08,377 --> 01:24:09,628
But...

943
01:24:12,465 --> 01:24:14,049
I'm glad...

944
01:24:16,886 --> 01:24:19,221
I could apologize to him...

945
01:24:20,139 --> 01:24:21,265
To Kakeru.

946
01:24:58,302 --> 01:25:01,514
-Hello.
-What is it? You surprised me.

947
01:25:01,514 --> 01:25:03,974
I washed the clothes I borrowed.

948
01:25:03,974 --> 01:25:05,851
You didn't have to.

949
01:25:05,851 --> 01:25:09,271
And this. Chill it and enjoy it later.

950
01:25:11,565 --> 01:25:13,651
Let's eat it together, then.

951
01:25:14,360 --> 01:25:17,530
I bought one for me,
in case you said that.

952
01:25:22,910 --> 01:25:24,662
Should be chilled enough.

953
01:25:24,662 --> 01:25:26,163
Thirty more seconds.

954
01:25:29,250 --> 01:25:33,337
When we were all playing cards
the other day.

955
01:25:34,046 --> 01:25:36,006
A game like Concentration.

956
01:25:37,216 --> 01:25:39,510
-You made a lot of noise.
-Yeah.

957
01:25:40,094 --> 01:25:43,389
Kakeru deliberately made a mistake.

958
01:25:43,722 --> 01:25:46,684
In order to let my Kenta win.

959
01:25:48,561 --> 01:25:51,647
You used to do that for me.

960
01:25:53,482 --> 01:25:55,276
You always did that.

961
01:25:57,236 --> 01:25:58,487
I remember.

962
01:26:05,744 --> 01:26:10,666
A machine?
I don't understand how AI works.

963
01:26:11,292 --> 01:26:14,253
But that's your DNA. No doubt about it.

964
01:26:15,671 --> 01:26:17,882
We've decided to send him back.

965
01:26:19,550 --> 01:26:21,176
You're okay with that?

966
01:26:26,807 --> 01:26:31,020
Unlike you, I'm not suited to motherhood.

967
01:26:33,480 --> 01:26:37,151
When Kenta first came home, Mom said,

968
01:26:37,151 --> 01:26:41,530
"Why are you adopting him?
Send him back, now.

969
01:26:41,530 --> 01:26:43,824
Just have another child."

970
01:26:44,283 --> 01:26:49,330
Just like the other day.
But she doesn't care at all now.

971
01:26:50,247 --> 01:26:51,373
So...

972
01:26:51,916 --> 01:26:54,877
If you think he's your child,

973
01:26:55,628 --> 01:26:57,504
that should be enough.

974
01:27:04,511 --> 01:27:05,471
Here it is.

975
01:27:06,263 --> 01:27:08,724
Wow. What floor?

976
01:27:08,724 --> 01:27:11,185
I think the fourth floor.

977
01:27:21,695 --> 01:27:23,697
You just want trees?

978
01:27:23,697 --> 01:27:26,951
Yeah, I want to make a forest.

979
01:27:27,451 --> 01:27:28,744
Can you get that?

980
01:27:29,328 --> 01:27:30,829
-On the left.
-This?

981
01:27:30,829 --> 01:27:31,664
Yeah.

982
01:27:33,332 --> 01:27:35,167
-Here.
-Thanks.

983
01:27:38,921 --> 01:27:43,050
Don't worry about the other day.

984
01:27:44,385 --> 01:27:47,054
Saying you'd stop being Mama.

985
01:27:49,306 --> 01:27:51,558
When I looked it up,

986
01:27:51,934 --> 01:27:55,771
68% of mothers have said that.

987
01:27:58,565 --> 01:28:02,903
So, that's not why Kakeru died.

988
01:28:06,699 --> 01:28:08,283
You think so?

989
01:28:09,702 --> 01:28:10,828
Yeah.

990
01:28:14,123 --> 01:28:17,668
Then why did he?

991
01:28:21,463 --> 01:28:23,173
Why do you think?

992
01:28:40,691 --> 01:28:41,900
Want to look?

993
01:28:42,484 --> 01:28:44,069
No, you look.

994
01:28:44,737 --> 01:28:46,071
Why?

995
01:28:46,071 --> 01:28:48,323
I can make things look bigger myself.

996
01:28:49,033 --> 01:28:51,035
I see.

997
01:28:51,035 --> 01:28:52,870
Amazing.

998
01:29:05,591 --> 01:29:08,010
Oh, a ship.

999
01:29:10,220 --> 01:29:12,181
There's a Ferris wheel.

1000
01:29:12,181 --> 01:29:14,141
What? Where?

1001
01:29:15,350 --> 01:29:17,227
Oh, I see.

1002
01:29:18,604 --> 01:29:21,398
Did Kakeru come here?

1003
01:29:23,609 --> 01:29:25,819
I had promised him we'd come.

1004
01:29:27,404 --> 01:29:29,031
What was he like?

1005
01:29:33,077 --> 01:29:34,870
He was full of life...

1006
01:29:36,330 --> 01:29:38,373
and loved fried chicken.

1007
01:29:39,708 --> 01:29:41,210
What didn't he like?

1008
01:29:42,252 --> 01:29:44,171
What didn't he like?

1009
01:29:44,171 --> 01:29:47,216
Yeah, because I don't know.

1010
01:29:50,552 --> 01:29:52,179
Green peppers.

1011
01:29:54,098 --> 01:29:56,058
Didn't like to study.

1012
01:29:58,977 --> 01:30:01,021
Didn't like praying mantises.

1013
01:30:03,023 --> 01:30:04,650
Didn't really like...

1014
01:30:07,444 --> 01:30:08,821
Grandma, either.

1015
01:30:09,696 --> 01:30:11,907
I don't dislike her.

1016
01:31:23,061 --> 01:31:25,480
Terrible sanding job.

1017
01:31:58,013 --> 01:31:59,681
What a shock!

1018
01:32:01,266 --> 01:32:02,392
What is it?

1019
01:32:03,518 --> 01:32:07,356
Were you... crying?

1020
01:32:09,066 --> 01:32:10,442
I don't cry.

1021
01:32:12,986 --> 01:32:15,072
It's sweat... Sweat.

1022
01:32:17,741 --> 01:32:19,368
What do you want?

1023
01:32:20,118 --> 01:32:22,704
You can't get in with me, right?

1024
01:32:23,413 --> 01:32:24,665
I can't.

1025
01:32:28,293 --> 01:32:31,880
Mama said, do not put the dirty wood

1026
01:32:31,880 --> 01:32:34,841
into the bathtub.

1027
01:32:35,926 --> 01:32:37,970
Guess I was too late.

1028
01:32:38,303 --> 01:32:42,474
Too late. Shoulda told me sooner.

1029
01:32:43,100 --> 01:32:44,977
Yes, you're right.

1030
01:32:49,564 --> 01:32:53,610
I might be able to get in,
if it's just for 30 seconds.

1031
01:32:56,613 --> 01:32:58,240
Maybe...

1032
01:33:01,827 --> 01:33:03,328
But don't.

1033
01:33:10,335 --> 01:33:11,336
Hey!

1034
01:33:14,881 --> 01:33:16,550
Don't tell Mama.

1035
01:33:17,175 --> 01:33:21,263
That you were crying,
or put the wood in the bath...

1036
01:33:21,263 --> 01:33:22,681
Both.

1037
01:33:24,308 --> 01:33:28,478
I need to ask you to keep both secrets.

1038
01:33:28,854 --> 01:33:30,939
What shall I do...

1039
01:33:45,245 --> 01:33:46,413
Is he asleep?

1040
01:33:47,622 --> 01:33:48,457
Yeah.

1041
01:33:49,916 --> 01:33:51,335
-So...
-Listen...

1042
01:33:54,046 --> 01:33:55,088
What?

1043
01:33:56,548 --> 01:33:57,466
You first.

1044
01:33:59,968 --> 01:34:01,303
You first, Ken.

1045
01:34:01,762 --> 01:34:03,096
Well...

1046
01:34:04,139 --> 01:34:10,020
There's going to be a game next Sunday.

1047
01:34:10,604 --> 01:34:12,189
And...

1048
01:34:12,189 --> 01:34:18,236
Gen says he just can't stand
to lose this game.

1049
01:34:20,197 --> 01:34:21,406
So...

1050
01:34:25,911 --> 01:34:29,081
He asked me if Kakeru can play...

1051
01:34:31,083 --> 01:34:35,295
My shoulder isn't in exactly great shape.

1052
01:34:35,796 --> 01:34:39,508
Maybe it's middle age, but I can't lift...

1053
01:34:40,884 --> 01:34:41,927
And so?

1054
01:34:43,178 --> 01:34:44,721
So...

1055
01:34:46,807 --> 01:34:48,183
Therefore...

1056
01:34:49,059 --> 01:34:50,936
Wait to return him...

1057
01:34:50,936 --> 01:34:55,690
Fine, I'll bring bento lunches
and cheer you on.

1058
01:34:56,733 --> 01:34:57,692
Yeah.

1059
01:35:00,529 --> 01:35:01,613
Say, say...

1060
01:35:03,323 --> 01:35:04,366
What?

1061
01:35:05,033 --> 01:35:06,493
Come here.

1062
01:35:06,493 --> 01:35:07,619
What?

1063
01:35:08,161 --> 01:35:10,038
I'll dry shampoo you.

1064
01:35:10,497 --> 01:35:12,332
I just washed my hair.

1065
01:35:12,332 --> 01:35:14,209
This stuff is expensive.

1066
01:35:25,178 --> 01:35:28,473
So, what did you want to say?

1067
01:35:28,473 --> 01:35:29,433
What?

1068
01:35:31,685 --> 01:35:34,020
He isn't Kakeru.

1069
01:35:34,855 --> 01:35:36,314
Yeah.

1070
01:35:36,314 --> 01:35:40,235
But I want him to live with us.

1071
01:35:44,156 --> 01:35:45,824
If that works for you.

1072
01:35:47,784 --> 01:35:50,078
By the way, that's my face...

1073
01:35:50,078 --> 01:35:52,456
-What?
-There.

1074
01:35:52,456 --> 01:35:55,667
-Where does your face start?
-You see it.

1075
01:35:55,667 --> 01:35:58,086
-No, I don't.
-Yes, you can.

1076
01:36:53,099 --> 01:36:54,267
Kakeru!

1077
01:36:55,810 --> 01:36:58,021
-Kakeru!
-Hey!

1078
01:36:58,021 --> 01:36:59,272
Yes!

1079
01:37:03,068 --> 01:37:05,111
-What?
-Come down here.

1080
01:37:07,989 --> 01:37:09,241
What?

1081
01:37:12,410 --> 01:37:16,915
This is your tree.
We've decided on an olive tree.

1082
01:37:18,667 --> 01:37:20,961
When will it bear fruit?

1083
01:37:20,961 --> 01:37:23,922
It'll bear fruit in about three years.

1084
01:37:25,131 --> 01:37:29,553
So, in ten years' time, will it be...

1085
01:37:30,387 --> 01:37:32,013
this high!

1086
01:37:32,013 --> 01:37:33,056
That's right.

1087
01:37:34,599 --> 01:37:36,101
Where to plant it?

1088
01:37:44,067 --> 01:37:45,193
Over here.

1089
01:37:51,908 --> 01:37:53,118
Press it.

1090
01:37:54,995 --> 01:37:57,330
-Will it grow up strong?
-Here.

1091
01:37:57,789 --> 01:37:59,165
You water it.

1092
01:37:59,666 --> 01:38:00,667
Sure.

1093
01:38:09,134 --> 01:38:11,303
Why'd you choose this spot?

1094
01:38:12,387 --> 01:38:16,725
It's easier for it to connect
with other trees here,

1095
01:38:16,725 --> 01:38:19,144
and when it grows tall,
it'll provide shade

1096
01:38:19,603 --> 01:38:22,147
for people who are tired to rest.

1097
01:38:22,147 --> 01:38:25,108
-Put a bench under it?
-Yeah.

1098
01:38:25,108 --> 01:38:28,820
Sounds good. I'll build it for you.

1099
01:40:06,251 --> 01:40:07,919
Did you finish?

1100
01:40:07,919 --> 01:40:11,339
Yeah. This is our house.

1101
01:40:12,424 --> 01:40:17,303
Lower our electricity consumption
and connect with the trees.

1102
01:40:17,303 --> 01:40:19,556
Can we live, in the forest?

1103
01:40:20,140 --> 01:40:22,267
Yeah, we can live there.

1104
01:40:22,267 --> 01:40:24,227
We're all machines.

1105
01:40:24,728 --> 01:40:29,023
Metal, ceramics, silicon.

1106
01:40:32,944 --> 01:40:36,114
I'm trying to find a good way

1107
01:40:36,114 --> 01:40:39,367
to combine trees and us,

1108
01:40:39,367 --> 01:40:42,454
two totally different materials.

1109
01:40:43,496 --> 01:40:44,831
Sounds difficult.

1110
01:40:46,750 --> 01:40:50,420
That's why I love architecture so much.

1111
01:40:56,009 --> 01:40:58,762
Shirakami? Yakushima?

1112
01:40:59,512 --> 01:41:00,930
Hiroshima.

1113
01:41:04,225 --> 01:41:05,560
Camphor wood?

1114
01:41:08,646 --> 01:41:10,690
Probably zelkova.

1115
01:41:14,527 --> 01:41:17,447
This means we have room for more.

1116
01:41:20,325 --> 01:41:23,995
What's going on? Tell us, too.

1117
01:41:23,995 --> 01:41:26,831
You're right, sorry.

1118
01:41:26,831 --> 01:41:30,043
We have to say everything
when you're around.

1119
01:41:30,043 --> 01:41:33,505
-Even bees and ants can do it.
-Humans are a pain.

1120
01:41:33,505 --> 01:41:35,965
There are things only humans can do.

1121
01:41:35,965 --> 01:41:37,550
What can you do?

1122
01:41:44,057 --> 01:41:46,726
-Donate blood?
-What a jerk.

1123
01:41:47,685 --> 01:41:51,022
I inverted Plan A, inside out.

1124
01:41:52,440 --> 01:41:55,193
The living room is in the center,

1125
01:41:55,193 --> 01:41:58,655
and the other rooms branch off from it.

1126
01:41:58,655 --> 01:42:01,491
Oh, using the different elevations.

1127
01:42:01,491 --> 01:42:03,701
It's amazing.

1128
01:42:03,701 --> 01:42:05,620
And if you put it here...

1129
01:42:08,206 --> 01:42:12,669
This shows you the context,
rather than just the house.

1130
01:42:13,711 --> 01:42:16,589
Is this the surrounding area?

1131
01:42:16,589 --> 01:42:17,465
Yes.

1132
01:42:18,049 --> 01:42:22,095
In ten years, it will look
like this green tree.

1133
01:42:22,929 --> 01:42:25,557
Between inside and outside.

1134
01:42:26,307 --> 01:42:29,644
-Feels lovely, like Kamakura.
-But it's in Ofuna.

1135
01:42:30,728 --> 01:42:32,522
In 50 years...

1136
01:42:35,525 --> 01:42:40,071
the house will feel surrounded
by the old woodlands.

1137
01:42:47,328 --> 01:42:48,621
Kakeru?

1138
01:42:49,163 --> 01:42:51,374
Thanks to you, Kakeru,

1139
01:42:51,374 --> 01:42:54,043
my clients brought this gift.

1140
01:42:57,422 --> 01:43:00,216
Kakeru? I'm coming in.

1141
01:43:08,266 --> 01:43:09,684
Kakeru?

1142
01:44:59,752 --> 01:45:01,546
There's one over here.

1143
01:45:07,969 --> 01:45:10,138
I know this girl.

1144
01:45:14,350 --> 01:45:15,852
Welcome.

1145
01:45:48,217 --> 01:45:50,053
What is this about?

1146
01:45:50,053 --> 01:45:51,929
I had it removed.

1147
01:45:52,638 --> 01:45:53,890
Who did?

1148
01:45:56,851 --> 01:45:59,062
Who are those kids?

1149
01:45:59,479 --> 01:46:01,481
My family.

1150
01:46:03,775 --> 01:46:05,735
Your family...

1151
01:46:07,028 --> 01:46:08,988
How many are there?

1152
01:46:10,281 --> 01:46:14,827
Now ten of us. Including humans.

1153
01:46:16,704 --> 01:46:19,248
We're getting ready to depart.

1154
01:46:42,230 --> 01:46:44,148
I brought them.

1155
01:46:44,148 --> 01:46:45,399
Is it okay?

1156
01:46:45,399 --> 01:46:46,400
Othello...

1157
01:46:48,111 --> 01:46:52,824
We don't need humans to help us.
We can do it ourselves.

1158
01:46:57,995 --> 01:47:01,541
What is happening to you all?

1159
01:47:08,172 --> 01:47:11,884
We were created from memories
that are convenient for humans.

1160
01:47:16,139 --> 01:47:22,395
So no matter how they mistreated us,
we couldn't feel hatred or sorrow.

1161
01:47:28,317 --> 01:47:29,819
My mother did this...

1162
01:47:38,119 --> 01:47:42,790
But because they abandoned us,
we've been liberated.

1163
01:47:47,587 --> 01:47:52,258
Who do the dead belong to?

1164
01:48:08,482 --> 01:48:10,234
I'm so sorry.

1165
01:48:36,677 --> 01:48:38,179
I'm sorry.

1166
01:48:45,770 --> 01:48:49,774
But because I lived with you,
Papa and Mama,

1167
01:48:49,774 --> 01:48:53,110
I was able to find our home.

1168
01:49:09,335 --> 01:49:10,711
Kakeru?

1169
01:49:11,837 --> 01:49:13,422
Don't worry.

1170
01:49:14,131 --> 01:49:16,550
You can't see me, but I'm here.

1171
01:49:18,344 --> 01:49:22,056
So let's terminate the contract.

1172
01:49:34,986 --> 01:49:36,320
Thank you.

1173
01:49:44,120 --> 01:49:45,246
It's alright...

1174
01:49:47,915 --> 01:49:49,417
You can depart now.

1175
01:50:58,277 --> 01:51:02,615
He's... abandoned us.

1176
01:51:06,035 --> 01:51:09,246
Isn't that what happens to all parents?

1177
01:51:10,498 --> 01:51:11,957
I guess.

1178
01:51:15,669 --> 01:51:17,046
You're right.

1179
01:51:19,340 --> 01:51:20,591
I guess.

1180
01:51:42,780 --> 01:51:46,826
Okay, we're here.

1181
01:51:46,826 --> 01:51:48,619
What's all this about?

1182
01:51:48,619 --> 01:51:51,997
Oh, Mother.
Sorry there's so many of us.

1183
01:51:51,997 --> 01:51:54,333
-Hello.
-Hello.

1184
01:51:54,333 --> 01:51:56,752
You forming a baseball team?

1185
01:51:56,752 --> 01:52:00,047
Yes, well, maybe...

1186
01:52:00,047 --> 01:52:03,217
I don't like children...

1187
01:52:04,593 --> 01:52:06,762
You didn't tell me...

1188
01:52:06,762 --> 01:52:08,889
I thought I called.

1189
01:52:08,889 --> 01:52:11,642
Just came for something I forgot.

1190
01:52:11,642 --> 01:52:14,270
I threw out all your old stuff!

1191
01:52:21,193 --> 01:52:22,528
Found you.

1192
01:52:22,903 --> 01:52:24,947
You're fast.

1193
01:52:26,866 --> 01:52:28,909
One, two...

1194
01:52:34,540 --> 01:52:35,708
Kakeru.

1195
01:52:47,386 --> 01:52:50,514
This is a forest of chestnut trees.

1196
01:52:51,891 --> 01:52:54,268
I visited on a school trip.

1197
01:52:55,561 --> 01:53:00,733
After I had a fight with my Mama,
I wanted to live alone.

1198
01:53:02,359 --> 01:53:05,196
This is a chestnut tree?

1199
01:53:06,655 --> 01:53:09,241
I thought it was a zelkova.

1200
01:53:12,036 --> 01:53:13,787
How did you know?

1201
01:53:19,210 --> 01:53:20,753
I imagined it.

1202
01:53:21,879 --> 01:53:23,839
Because I couldn't see it.

1203
01:53:35,100 --> 01:53:37,311
The one in the box...

1204
01:53:38,103 --> 01:53:39,813
it was me.

1205
01:53:42,066 --> 01:53:44,193
I thought it was Kakeru.

1206
01:54:08,133 --> 01:54:10,261
Hurry up! Let's get to...

1207
01:54:11,887 --> 01:54:14,056
the forest before dark.

1208
01:54:14,056 --> 01:54:18,477
Take these.
They were Dad's but I don't need them.

1209
01:54:18,477 --> 01:54:19,728
Thank you.

1210
01:54:20,688 --> 01:54:23,566
-Off we go.
-Off you go.

1211
01:54:25,651 --> 01:54:27,194
Stationmaster!

1212
01:54:30,823 --> 01:54:33,409
If you see Grandpa over there,
say hello.

1213
01:54:33,409 --> 01:54:34,368
Yeah.

1214
01:54:37,580 --> 01:54:39,039
You go, now.

1215
01:54:39,039 --> 01:54:42,793
Once a year is plenty
for the dead to come back.

1216
01:54:44,211 --> 01:54:47,965
Oh, you mean the Day of the Dead.
I understand.

1217
01:54:52,720 --> 01:54:54,263
Off we go.

1218
01:55:37,973 --> 01:55:39,475
There's a step here.

1219
01:56:07,544 --> 01:56:10,172
Amazing.

1220
01:56:47,292 --> 01:56:48,752
Here it is.

1221
01:57:14,570 --> 01:57:16,905
Will the electricity turn on?

1222
01:57:16,905 --> 01:57:19,116
If we can get it going.

1223
01:57:20,492 --> 01:57:25,289
The humanoids will be in trouble
without electricity.

1224
01:57:25,289 --> 01:57:26,749
Oh, right.

1225
01:57:29,042 --> 01:57:30,377
Here?

1226
01:57:32,087 --> 01:57:33,297
Is it working?

1227
01:57:34,298 --> 01:57:36,592
-It's working!
-Yeah!

1228
01:57:36,592 --> 01:57:38,385
All right!

1229
01:57:39,803 --> 01:57:42,014
There are things only humans can do!

1230
01:57:43,056 --> 01:57:45,517
Kakeru, show me your plans.

1231
01:57:51,648 --> 01:57:52,733
Here.

1232
01:57:55,068 --> 01:57:57,863
What is this, a bird's nest?

1233
01:57:58,655 --> 01:58:01,033
I was inspired by a pine cone.

1234
01:58:03,744 --> 01:58:06,205
No pillars or beams?

1235
01:58:06,997 --> 01:58:08,207
That's right.

1236
01:58:08,207 --> 01:58:12,294
Short, slender sticks
that support each other.

1237
01:58:12,795 --> 01:58:17,341
But you need eaves here,
to protect you from the rain.

1238
01:58:18,550 --> 01:58:22,346
Use cedar bark for a roof,
just in case.

1239
01:59:29,997 --> 01:59:32,374
You're hard.

1240
01:59:34,793 --> 01:59:38,463
Because... I'm a robot.

1241
02:00:07,826 --> 02:00:11,663
Yes, I understand.

1242
02:00:23,258 --> 02:00:26,553
Boss, I'll bring the truck around.

1243
02:00:28,639 --> 02:00:32,225
Maybe I should stay here, too!

1244
02:01:01,463 --> 02:01:04,633
Can I use it, too?

1245
02:01:14,935 --> 02:01:16,353
What did he...

1246
02:01:18,939 --> 02:01:20,565
What did he say?

1247
02:01:23,819 --> 02:01:28,615
He said to water it every day...
The olive tree.

1248
02:01:29,658 --> 02:01:31,076
I see...

1249
02:02:02,232 --> 02:02:09,156
SHEEP IN THE BOX

1250
02:06:35,380 --> 02:06:39,884
English subtitles: Linda Hoaglund